  <div id="sutta">
  
              <h2 id="SN2.17" class="h1">Subrahma the Deva’s Son<br/>
                Subrahma Sutta  (SN 2:17)</h2>
<p>Standing to one side, Subrahma the deva’s son recited this verse to the Blessed One:</p>
<div class="verse">
<p>“Constantly troubled is this mind,</p>
<p>constantly trembling this heart,</p>
<p class="v2">over duties<span class="fn"><a href="#sn2.17note01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--> that have yet to arise,</p>
<p class="v2">and those already arisen.</p>
<p>If there is an Untroubled,</p>
<p class="v2">point it out to me, when asked.”</p>
<div class="verse-add">
<p class="vspk">The Buddha:</p>
<p>“Apart from the factors for awakening, austerity,<span class="fn"><a href="#sn2.17note02">2</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
<p>apart from sense-faculty restraint,</p>
<p>apart from relinquishing the all,<span class="fn"><a href="#sn2.17note03">3</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
<p class="v2">I see no safety</p>
<p class="v2">for living beings.”</p>
</div><!--end verse-add-->
</div><!--end verse-->
<p>That is what the Blessed One said… the deva disappeared right there.</p>
              <div class="note">
                <p class="notetitle">Notes</p><!--end notetitle-->
                <p id="sn2.17note01">1. Reading <em>kiccesu</em> with the Thai version. The Burmese version has <em>kicchesu,</em> “troubles.”</p>
                <p id="sn2.17note02">2. Reading <em>bojjhaṅga-tapasā</em> with the Thai version. The Burmese version has <em>bojjhā tapasā,</em> “from awakening, from austerity.” The Commentary notes that <em>bojjhaṅga/bojjhā</em> refers to the development of the factors of awakening (see <a href="../MN/MN118.html">MN 118</a>). <em>Tapasā,</em> it says, refers to undertaking the dhutaṅga practices (see <a href="../KN/Thag/thag16_7.html">Thag 16:7</a>).</p>
                <p id="sn2.17note03">3. On the definition of the all, see <a href="SN35_23.html">SN 35:23</a>.</p>
              </div><!--end notes-->

      </div><!--end:sutta-->
    </div><!--end:content-->
    
